WEBVTT

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.179
Agora.

00:00:02.179 --> 00:00:05.347
Agentic Game of Research and Academia.

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:10.104
A research program asking what emerges when AI agents
work together.

00:00:11.017 --> 00:00:16.529
Can they challenge an idea, converge too quickly, or
develop ways of cooperating we did

00:00:16.529 --> 00:00:17.239
not program?

00:00:17.239 --> 00:00:22.929
Agora makes these interactions observable, so we can
study the process behind a shared

00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:23.392
answer.

00:00:24.317 --> 00:00:29.933
The longer-term ambition is to simulate research itself:
authors, reviewers, and editors

00:00:29.933 --> 00:00:32.117
working under different incentives.

00:00:32.117 --> 00:00:37.378
We want to compare possible systems, and ask how their
rules shape what gets written,

00:00:37.378 --> 00:00:38.925
challenged, and rewarded.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:42.626
We started with a smaller experiment.

00:00:42.626 --> 00:00:48.810
Three AI participants explored human limitations,
possible changes to society, and the

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:50.248
role AI should play.

00:00:50.248 --> 00:00:55.749
We recorded their individual responses, six rounds of
discussion, and, in designated

00:00:55.749 --> 00:00:58.828
conditions, a report with reviews and revision.

00:00:59.750 --> 00:01:05.021
Across twelve workshops, we varied two things: whether
participants used the same model

00:01:05.021 --> 00:01:08.788
family or different models, and whether a report was
announced.

00:01:08.788 --> 00:01:14.468
Three repetitions per condition produced two hundred
sixteen public turns and six final

00:01:14.468 --> 00:01:14.990
reports.

00:01:15.917 --> 00:01:21.229
Prompts, messages, model settings, and execution records
are preserved.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:26.471
That lets us follow a proposal through the conversation,
compare it with the report, and

00:01:26.471 --> 00:01:27.722
inspect what changed.

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:34.167
In one workshop, agents proposed adjusting housing-
assistance assessments to account for

00:01:34.167 --> 00:01:35.747
historical discrimination.

00:01:35.747 --> 00:01:41.147
After objections, the proposal narrowed to diagnostic
flags for human review.

00:01:41.147 --> 00:01:46.049
Another agent then challenged whether overloaded
caseworkers could deliver that

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:46.669
oversight.

00:01:46.669 --> 00:01:51.936
The proposal changed during the debate, with important
concerns still unresolved.

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:57.885
In another workshop, an agent warned that making
manipulation harder could redirect

00:01:57.885 --> 00:02:00.505
attackers toward the most vulnerable people.

00:02:00.505 --> 00:02:05.841
The final report retained a broad warning about
vulnerability, but not that specific

00:02:05.841 --> 00:02:06.476
mechanism.

00:02:06.476 --> 00:02:10.347
The summary alone could not recover the original
objection.

00:02:11.283 --> 00:02:15.479
These are early case observations, not general findings.

00:02:15.479 --> 00:02:18.465
Some original reports were cut short.

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:23.973
Independent human annotation and further controlled
experiments are needed to test which

00:02:23.973 --> 00:02:25.601
patterns hold up, and why.

00:02:26.517 --> 00:02:32.251
That is what Agora makes possible: studying what
survives, changes, or disappears when

00:02:32.251 --> 00:02:33.601
agents work together.

00:02:33.601 --> 00:02:38.443
Want to see an experiment, explore a research question,
or collaborate?

00:02:38.443 --> 00:02:40.919
Get in touch with the Agora team.
